0 Automatic Binding Brick, 1949, 2 x 2 - the third brick (A3-S04)
0 Name: lego.ldr
0 Author: spex project (hand-authored placement data; not sourced from an official ldraw.org models/ release)
0 // Places `parts/abb-2x2.dat` - the SAME brick as A3-S03's in every dimension
0 // and in its tapered end slots, with sharp vertical edges and flat stud tops.
0 //
0 // That is not a modelling shortcut, it is the finding. The Brighton Toy and
0 // Model Index: "Like the Page bricks, the first Lego bricks had slotted ends
0 // with a tapered slot, and their dimensions were pretty much indistinguishable
0 // from Page's - they were quite clearly copies" - and Lego dropped the
0 // rounded vertical edges and flattened the stud tops, which years later
0 // became the space for the logo.
0 //
0 // So the copy kept everything that was about the SYSTEM and left behind the
0 // two things that were about the HAND. A macro of this brick beside a macro
0 // of Page's is the whole of A3-S03 and A3-S04 without a caption having to
0 // say it.
0 //
0 // Still hollow, still no tube: 1958 is the next shot.
0 //
0 // Brick height 24 LDU, origin at the footprint centre, LDraw +Y down: a
0 // brick standing on the ground plane is translated to y = -24.
1 15 20240 -24 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 abb-2x2.dat
